From b5c69f5b7b90fefc2269180cd86a35050bf80e21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 19:59:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 095/115] arm64: dts: qcom: msm8998-google-wahoo: describe the
 measured CPU capacity

CPU frequency scaling does not work on this SoC yet, so both clusters
run at whatever the bootloader left them at: silver near its maximum,
gold at about a sixth of its rated clock. The capacity model still
describes the rated ratio, 1536 for gold against 1024 for silver, so
the scheduler prefers the cluster that is in fact 2.6 times slower and
pays a cluster migration to get there.

A fixed integer loop measures 4.16 s pinned to silver, 10.99 s pinned
to gold and 22.68 s unpinned -- the unpinned case is worse than either
cluster on its own. Describing gold at its measured capacity,
1024 * 4.16 / 10.99 = 388, brings the unpinned case to 4.65 s.

Revert this once the OSM is programmed and cpufreq reports both
policies: with scaling working gold is genuinely the faster cluster,
and this override would then invert the model in the other direction.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 .../boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-wahoo.dtsi   | 22 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-wahoo.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-wahoo.dtsi
index be6edb5d0c36..7a4e3840cff1 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-wahoo.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-wahoo.dtsi
@@ -681,3 +681,25 @@ &pmi8998_charger {
 
 	monitored-battery = <&wahoo_batt>;
 };
+
+/*
+ * Until CPU frequency scaling works on this SoC, the gold cluster runs at
+ * whatever frequency the bootloader left it at -- roughly a sixth of its
+ * rated clock -- while silver runs near its own maximum. The capacity model
+ * inherited from msm8998.dtsi describes the *rated* ratio (1536 vs 1024), so
+ * the scheduler treats the slow cluster as the fast one, places every wakeup
+ * there and migrates across the boundary for nothing.
+ *
+ * Measured on taimen with a fixed integer loop: 4.16 s pinned to silver,
+ * 10.99 s pinned to gold, 22.68 s unpinned. Describing gold at its measured
+ * capacity instead, 1024 * 4.16 / 10.99 = 388, brings the unpinned case to
+ * 4.65 s.
+ *
+ * This is contingent on cpufreq being absent and must be dropped as soon as
+ * the OSM is programmed: with scaling working, gold really is the faster
+ * cluster and this override would invert the model the other way.
+ */
+&cpu4 { capacity-dmips-mhz = <388>; };
+&cpu5 { capacity-dmips-mhz = <388>; };
+&cpu6 { capacity-dmips-mhz = <388>; };
+&cpu7 { capacity-dmips-mhz = <388>; };
